      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Stretching the sciatic nerve for the relief of \"sciatica\" was frequently employed before 1900 and was subsequently abandoned, probably because it was done without sufficient scrutiny of the indications. The procedure has recently been employed in cases in which \"sciatica\" remains following the operative removal of ruptured intervertebral discs, and it has been instrumental in relieving postoperative \"sciatica\" when the cause was the formation of adhesions about the lumbar nerve roots. If the nerve root is compressed by recurrent disc protrusion or by adjacent bone, the manipulation usually increases the pain, a phenomenon that has been helpful from a diagnostic standpoint."},
